{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788202526,"nonce":"UKs/hdcOV06aeZ8+kIIxTHOhpGU=","message":"VERIFICATION (backlog verification pass, git-bug 2f2796e, run fbb2c59)\n\nCONFIRMED — every claim in this issue is about the project's own design rather\nthan about the game, and each one resolves. Two additions and one dependency\ncorrection.\n\nWHAT I CONFIRMED BY READING\n\n- \"4.1 defines the escalation ladder (prose lesson -\u003e checklist line -\u003e\n  template/policy)\" — CONFIRMED against `96d9315`, whose `postmortem.md` bullet\n  says \"escalation ladder (prose -\u003e checklist -\u003e template) with criteria for\n  promotion\", and whose `playbook/` bullet defines the per-lesson frontmatter\n  this spec's \"trigger metadata\" extends.\n- The DESIGN parallel is exact. DESIGN §Learning: \"Lessons are earned via journal\n  post-mortems and escalate in strength: prose lesson -\u003e checklist item -\u003e baked\n  into templates/policies (the power-room template grows a firefoam popper)\". So\n  the firefoam-popper example in this issue is DESIGN's own canonical case, used\n  correctly. CONFIRMED.\n- The digest-budget analogy is accurate and worth leaning on harder than the\n  issue does. DESIGN §Observation model, on the measured digest budget: \"What\n  makes it a budget rather than a hope is that every list-valued section is\n  capped and reports what it dropped; **an uncapped section is the defect, not a\n  large number**.\" That sentence is the argument, and it carries one more clause\n  than this issue borrows — *reports what it dropped*. A capped checklist that\n  silently omits the overflow is exactly the failure the digest rule was written\n  against. Add it: the checklist file names what was merged or retired to make\n  room, in the file, not only in a commit message.\n- `state:backlog`, `priority:p3`, `wave:4` and the dependency on 4.1 are all\n  consistent with 4.1 being `state:backlog wave:4` too. CONFIRMED.\n\nCORRECTION — THE DEPENDENCY LINE UNDERSTATES ONE AND OVERSTATES ANOTHER\n\nThe header says \"escalation step additionally wants 5.1\". Two things:\n\n- **5.1 is further away than \"additionally wants\" suggests.** `7c83d6c` is open,\n  `rwtest/` does not exist in the repo at all, and its own acceptance depends on a\n  bench-version mechanism that does not exist anywhere (see the verification\n  comment there). So the fourth bullet is not \"one more dep\" but \"blocked behind a\n  spec that is itself blocked on undefined work\". The issue already handles this\n  correctly in its last acceptance bullet — \"may close with the first three\n  bullets and file the rwtest half onto 5.1 if 5.1 is not yet done — say so in\n  those words rather than waiting\" — which is the right call and is unusually\n  well phrased. Keep it; just do not read the header as though 5.1 were near.\n- **4.2 is a real dependency and is not listed.** The second acceptance bullet is\n  \"The cap and the ledger are readable by the play-loop (4.2) without parsing\n  prose\". That is a contract ON 4.2's session-start format, and 4.2 (`d2e1229`)\n  is open with its own \"Session start: load playbook INDEX + checklists\" bullet\n  unwritten. Whichever lands second inherits the other's format. Say so, the way\n  comment #3 on 4.1 already says this spec's constraints are contract for 4.1's\n  checklist format — that same sentence is owed in the other direction.\n\nADDITION 1 — THE LEDGER HAS A NATURAL SOURCE AND NOBODY HAS NAMED IT\n\n\"Retirement evidence. Each checklist line carries trigger metadata (what journal\nsignal or condition it watches, when it last actually fired).\"\n\n\"When it last actually fired\" cannot be maintained by hand across runs — that is\nprecisely the kind of bookkeeping that decays first. It CAN be derived, because\n4.2's run artifacts are the natural source: if the morning-checklist artifact is\none line per item ID with a verdict (which is what 4.2's own invariant\n\"checklist items always produce either 'ok' or an action; silent skips forbidden\"\nrequires in order to be checkable at all — I made the same recommendation on\n`d2e1229`), then \"last fired\" is a grep over `RUNS/*/`, not a field someone\nremembers to update.\n\nThat converts this spec's hardest bullet from a discipline into a computation.\nRecommend the two specs agree on ONE artifact format: item id, verdict, and the\ntick or run it fired in. 4.2 needs it to prove its invariant; 4.4 needs it to\ncompute retirement. Neither can get it alone.\n\nADDITION 2 — \"PER-COLONY OR GLOBAL\" IS THE ONE OPEN QUESTION WITH A CONCRETE\nANSWER AVAILABLE\n\nThe open question: \"whether retirement is per-colony or global (a desert lesson\nidles on an ice sheet without being wrong).\"\n\nThere is already a vocabulary for this in the same ladder: 4.1's per-lesson\nfrontmatter has a `trigger-context` field. A lesson whose trigger-context is\n`biome:desert` is not a candidate for retirement on an ice-sheet run; it is\nINAPPLICABLE, which is a third disposition beside \"fired\" and \"did not fire\". Two\ncounters, not one — fired / not-fired-while-applicable / not-applicable — and\nonly the middle one accrues retirement pressure. That falls straight out of the\nfield 4.1 already defines and it dissolves the question rather than answering it.\nRecommending it rather than deciding it, since it is 4.1's field and this is not\n4.1.\n\nACCEPTANCE — TESTABLE, WITH ONE UNCHECKABLE WORD AND ONE ORDERING PROBLEM\n\n- \"Demonstrated on REAL playbook content from actual runs, not hypotheticals\" is\n  the right bar and it is also a **hard ordering constraint the issue does not\n  flag**: `playbook/` does not exist yet (4.1 authors it) and there are no run\n  artifacts yet (4.3 produces the first). So this acceptance cannot be attempted\n  until 4.1 has landed AND at least one M1 run has happened. That makes 4.4\n  strictly after 4.3, not merely after 4.1. Worth stating, because `priority:p3`\n  plus `wave:4` currently reads as \"any time in wave 4\".\n- \"at least one over-cap add forces a recorded merge/retire\" — testable, and it\n  needs the checklist to already be AT the cap, which on a first real playbook it\n  will not be. Either the demonstration sets N deliberately low for the exercise\n  (fine, if said), or it waits for enough real content. Say which.\n- \"at least one graduation deletes its checklist line in the same commit\" —\n  testable, mechanically, as a diff check. Good bullet.\n- \"at least one line is flagged by the retirement pass and dispositioned\" —\n  testable once Addition 1's artifact exists; not before, because there is\n  nothing to compute \"has not fired across N runs\" from.\n- \"The cap and the ledger are readable by the play-loop (4.2) without parsing\n  prose\" — testable, and the phrase \"without parsing prose\" is doing the real\n  work here: it forces a structured file. Keep the wording.\n- \"A worked example of the final escalation exists once 5.1 lands (may close with\n  the first three bullets ... — say so in those words rather than waiting)\" —\n  this is the best-drafted acceptance clause in the backlog. It names the partial\n  close, names the condition, and names the artifact. No change.\n\nWHAT I AM INFERRING, NOT CONFIRMING\n\n- Everything in Additions 1 and 2 is a recommendation about a format that does\n  not exist yet, not a finding about anything that does.\n- That 4.2 will produce a machine-readable checklist artifact. It currently does\n  not specify one; I have recommended it on both issues, and if neither takes it,\n  this spec's third bullet has no data source.","files":null}]}